  "content": "Through whatever virtue I have gainedBy composing this \"Engaging in the Bodhisattva Deeds,\"May all wandering beingsEnter into the conduct of enlightenment.In all directions, however many there may beWho suffer from diseases of body and mind,May they obtain an ocean of happiness and joyThrough these merits of mine.For as long as they remain in cyclic existence,May they never decline from happiness;May wandering beings attainThe uninterrupted bliss of the Bodhisattvas.In all the world systems, however many hells there may be,And whatever beings exist within them,May those embodied beingsRejoice in the bliss of Sukhāvatī.May those tormented by cold find warmth;And may those tormented by heat be cooledBy the infinite rivers flowingFrom the great clouds of the Bodhisattvas.May the forest of sword-leaves alsoBecome for them a dense sandalwood grove;May the Shalmali trees likewiseGrow into wish-fulfilling trees.May it be beautified by the melodious calls of ducks, ruddy geese, swans,And cranes, and may the regions of hell become delightfulWith lakes possessing the great fragrance of lotuses,Bringing joy to all who dwell there.May those heaps of glowing coals become heaps of jewels,And the scorched earth be spread as a crystal floor;May the mountains of the Crushing Hell become celestial palacesOf offerings, filled with Sugatas.May the rain of embers, burning stones, and weaponsFrom this moment on become a rain of flowers;And may those who strike one another with weaponsHenceforth toss flowers in play.May those submerged in the fordless river, like a pit of fire,Their flesh decayed and skeletons the color of kunda flowers,Attain divine bodies through the power of my merit,And dwell with goddesses by the gently flowing waters.Why do the henchmen of Yama, the hawks, and the terrible vultures cower in fear here?Thinking, \"Whose noble power is this that dispels darkness everywhere and generates blissful joy?\"They look upward and behold the blazing Vajrapāṇi dwelling in the midst of the sky;Through the force of their intense joy, may they be freed from sin and go together with him.As a rain of flowers falls, mingled with scented water,Seeing the glowing embers of hell extinguished with a hiss,They wonder, \"What is this that has suddenly satiated us with bliss?\"And thus may the hell-beings behold Padmapāṇi.\"Friends, cast away your fear and come here quickly to our side!By whose power is all suffering dispelled and a surge of joy attained?In whom have the Bodhicitta and compassion that protect all beings been born?Who is this radiant youth with five topknots who has arrived to grant us fearlessness?\"The crowns of a hundred gods offer worship to his lotus feet,And upon his head, moist with compassion, a rain of many flowers falls;\"Look!\" they cry, \"at this lovely palace where thousands of goddesses resound with songs of praise,\"Having seen Mañjughoṣa in such a way, may the denizens of hell now raise a cry of joy.Thus, through my roots of virtue, may they see clouds of unobstructed Bodhisattvas such as Samantabhadra,Raining down bliss and cool, fragrant showers;Upon seeing this,May those hell-beings be filled with manifest joy.May animals be free from the fear of preying upon one another;May they be free from the fear of being eaten;Just like the people of the Northern Continent,May the hungry ghosts become happy.By the stream of milk flowing from the hand of the noble Lord Avalokiteśvara,May the hungry ghosts be satisfied,And by bathing in it,May they be forever cooled.May the blind see forms,And may the deaf always hear sounds;Just like the goddess Māyādevī,May pregnant women also give birth without harm.May those who are naked obtain clothing,May those who are hungry find food,May those who are thirsty obtain water,And may they find delicious drinks.May the destitute obtain wealth,May those afflicted by grief find joy,May the despondent be refreshed,And may they attain perfect steadfastness.May all sentient beings who are sick,Be swiftly freed from their illnesses,May every disease of living beings,Never occur again.May those who are terrified be fearless,May those who are bound be liberated,May the powerless be endowed with power,And may their hearts be kind toward one another.For all travelers,May every direction be auspicious,And may whatever purpose they travel for,Be accomplished without effort.May those who have boarded boats and great ships,Have the intentions of their minds fulfilled,And having safely reached the water's shore,May they rejoice together with their kinsmen.May those who have strayed onto desolate paths,Meet with fellow travelers,And without fear of robbers, tigers, and the like,May they journey easily without fatigue.In desolate, pathless, and perilous places,Children, the elderly, and the helpless,Those asleep, intoxicated, or utterly deranged,May the gods provide them protection.May they be liberated from all hardships,Endowed with faith, wisdom, and compassion,And with perfect food and conduct,May they always remember their past lives.May all have inexhaustible enjoyments,Just like the treasury of space,And without conflict or violence,May they live with complete freedom.May those sentient beings who have little splendorCome to possess great splendor.May those ascetics who are physically unattractiveBecome endowed with perfect, beautiful forms.May all the women in the worldBecome men.May those of lowly status attain high positions,And may their pride be utterly subdued.By this merit of mine,May all sentient beings without exception,Having abandoned all misdeeds,Always engage in virtue.May they never be separated from the mind of enlightenment,May they be devoted to the conduct of awakening,May they be cared for by the Buddhas,And may they abandon the activities of Māra.May all those sentient beingsHave lives of immeasurable length;May they always live in happiness,And may even the word \"death\" never be heard.May all directions be filled with groves of wish-fulfilling trees,And be crowded with Buddhas and the sons of the Buddhas,Resounding with the sweet sound of the Dharma,So that all directions are completely filled.May the ground everywhereBe free from pebbles and the like,As level as the palm of a hand,And remain smooth in its nature, like lapis lazuli.In all the various circles of retinues,May many Bodhisattvas dwell,Adorning the surface of the earthWith their own excellence.May all embodied beings continuously hearThe sound of the Dharma from the birds,From the trees, from all rays of light,And even from the sky itself.May they always meet with the BuddhasAnd the sons of the Buddhas,And with infinite clouds of offerings,May they worship the Teachers of the world.May the gods also send rain at the proper time,And may the harvests be bountiful.May kings act in accordance with the Dharma,And may the world also flourish.May medicines be potent,And may the recitation of secret mantras be successful.May ḍākinīs, rākṣasas, and the like,Be endowed with a mind of compassion.May no sentient being ever suffer,May they not be afraid or held in contempt,And may no one ever be unhappy.May the temples flourish with study,And remain well-established through recitation.May the Sangha always be in harmony,And may the purposes of the Sangha be accomplished.May those monastics who desire to train,Also obtain the state of solitude.Having abandoned all distractions,May they meditate with a workable mind.May the nuns be endowed with requisites,And may they abandon quarreling and harm.Likewise, may all who have gone forthNever let their ethical discipline decline.Having become disillusioned by any lapse in discipline,May they always exhaust their misdeeds.Having attained a higher rebirth,May their spiritual conduct not decline even there.May the wise be honored,And may they also receive alms.May their mental continuums be completely purified,And may their fame spread in all directions.Without experiencing the suffering of the lower realms,And without undergoing hardships,Having obtained a body superior to the gods,May they swiftly attain Buddhahood.May all sentient beings repeatedlyMake offerings to all the Buddhas,And through the inconceivable bliss of the Buddhas,May they always be endowed with happiness.May the Bodhisattvas'Intentions to benefit wandering beings be fulfilled.Whatever the Protectors have intended,May that be bestowed upon all sentient beings.Likewise, may the Pratyekabuddhas andThe Shravakas also be at ease.Through the kindness of Mañjughoṣa, until I attainThe Ground of Supreme Joy,May I always remember my past lives, andMay I obtain the state of a renunciate.Even on a diet of simple food,May I live endowed with strength.In all my lives, may I findPerfect circumstances for living in solitude.Whenever I wish to see him, orEven wish to ask the slightest question,May I behold the Protector Mañjughoṣa himselfWithout any hindrance.For the sake of accomplishing the welfare of all sentient beingsReaching to the very limits of space in the ten directions,Just as Mañjughoṣa acts,May my conduct also be like that.For as long as space remains,And for as long as sentient beings remain,Until then, may I too remainTo dispel the suffering of the world.Whatever the sufferings of wandering beings may be,May they all ripen upon me.Through the assembly of Bodhisattvas,May all beings enjoy happiness.The sole medicine for the suffering of beings,The source from which all happiness arises,May the teachings, together with support and honor,Endure for a very long time.Through whose kindness a virtuous mind arises,To Mañjughoṣa I prostrate.Through whose kindness I have flourished,To the spiritual friend I also prostrate.This is the tenth chapter, titled \"Dedication,\" from the *Bodhicaryāvatāra*.",
